      "text": "To solve for the \\(2 \\times 2\\) matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) given the conditions \\(\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ 21 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -17 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we start by expressing \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) in terms of its general form:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we use the given condition \\(\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ 21 \\end{pmatrix}\\). This translates to:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3a \\\\ 3c \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ 21 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nFrom this, we get two equations:\n\\[\n3a = 6 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 3c = 21\n\\]\nSolving these, we find:\n\\[\na = 2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad c = 7\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the second given condition \\(\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -17 \\end{pmatrix}\\). This translates to:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -a + 5b \\\\ -c + 5d \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -17 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(a = 2\\) and \\(c = 7\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -2 + 5b \\\\ -7 + 5d \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -17 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nFrom this, we get two more equations:\n\\[\n-2 + 5b = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad -7 + 5d = -17\n\\]\nSolving these, we find:\n\\[\n5b = 5 \\implies b = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 5d = -10 \\implies d = -2\n\\]\n\nThus, the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
