      "text": "To find the \\(2 \\times 2\\) matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\), we start by letting \\(\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\\). We are given two equations based on the conditions provided:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ 21 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3a \\\\ 3c \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ 21 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFrom this, we can deduce that:\n\n\\[\n3a = 6 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 3c = 21\n\\]\n\nSolving these equations, we get:\n\n\\[\na = 2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad c = 7\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the second condition:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -17 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -a + 5b \\\\ -c + 5d \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -17 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSince we know \\(a = 2\\) and \\(c = 7\\), we substitute these values in:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -2 + 5b \\\\ -7 + 5d \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -17 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two more equations:\n\n\\[\n-2 + 5b = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad -7 + 5d = -17\n\\]\n\nSolving these equations, we get:\n\n\\[\n5b = 5 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad b = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n5d = -10 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad d = -2\n\\]\n\nThus, the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
